<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Cantor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Georg Ferdinand Ludwig Philipp Cantor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Cantor was a mathematician, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1845 and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, who did much to create set theory. He established the importance of one-to-one correspondence between the members of two sets, defined infinite and well-ordered sets, proved that the real numbers are more numerous than the natural numbers, and defined the cardinal and ordinal numbers and their arithmetic. His theory of transfinite numbers met resistance from contemporaries such as Leopold Kronecker and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare">Henri Poincaré</a>, and he suffered recurring bouts of depression from 1884 until his death in 1918. In 1904 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> awarded him its Sylvester Medal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-cantor.jpg" alt="Georg Cantor" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_Cantor_(Porträt).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cantor was born on 3 March 1845 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and lived there until he was eleven. He was the eldest of six children and an outstanding violinist. His father, Georg Waldemar Cantor, was Danish and worked as a broker on the Saint Petersburg stock exchange, and his mother, Maria Anna Böhm, was born in the city to an Austro-Hungarian musical family. After his father fell ill in 1856 they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Cantor studied in Zurich from 1862 and, after an inheritance on his father's death in 1863, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> under Leopold Kronecker, Karl Weierstrass and Ernst Kummer. He took his doctorate in 1867 and spent his whole career at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He turned from number theory to analysis at the suggestion of Eduard Heine and in 1869 solved the problem of the uniqueness of the representation of a function by a trigonometric series, where he found transfinite ordinals. His 1874 paper on the real algebraic numbers is often taken to mark the beginning of set theory. It gave the first rigorous proof that there is more than one kind of infinity, showing that the real numbers cannot be put in one-to-one correspondence with the positive integers. Letters between Cantor and Richard Dedekind show that the proof of the countability of the algebraic numbers came from Dedekind, whom Cantor did not acknowledge.</p><p>Cantor became a full professor in 1879 but wanted a chair at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Kronecker, who headed mathematics there until 1891, thought set theory asserted the existence of sets without constructing them and viewed Cantor as a corrupter of youth, and Cantor's applications were declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cantor was hospitalised in 1884 and 1899. In 1904 Julius König presented a paper attempting to disprove transfinite set theory. Ernst Zermelo showed within a day that it failed, but Cantor felt publicly humiliated. He suffered chronic depression afterwards and was repeatedly confined to sanatoria. He retired in 1913, lived in poverty during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and died of a heart attack in a sanatorium on 6 January 1918.</p><p>Cantor, a devout Lutheran, believed God had communicated the theory of transfinite numbers to him. Constructivists such as Kronecker, and intuitionists such as L. E. J. Brouwer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare">Henri Poincaré</a>, rejected actual infinity, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Ludwig Wittgenstein</a> attacked the theory later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hilbert">David Hilbert</a> defended it: no one, he said, would expel mathematicians from the paradise Cantor had created. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> gave him its Sylvester Medal in 1904.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georg Cantor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
